{
  "id": "a27fd487-85b4-4f18-b0ab-4be63db69f43",
  "kind": "Theorem",
  "definition_keys": [],
  "heading": null,
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{Uniqueness of Inverses}",
  "body_text": null,
  "proof_text": "Suppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ both satisfy the equations for an inverse of $x$:\n$x * y_1 = e = y_1 * x$ and $x * y_2 = e = y_2 * x$.\nUsing the identity property and associativity:\n$$ y_1 = y_1 * e = y_1 * (x * y_2) = (y_1 * x) * y_2 = e * y_2 = y_2. $$\nThus any two elements satisfying the inverse equations must coincide.",
  "proof_source": "Proof: \"\"\"\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ both satisfy the equations for an inverse of $x$:\n$x * y_1 = e = y_1 * x$ and $x * y_2 = e = y_2 * x$.\nUsing the identity property and associativity:\n$$ y_1 = y_1 * e = y_1 * (x * y_2) = (y_1 * x) * y_2 = e * y_2 = y_2. $$\nThus any two elements satisfying the inverse equations must coincide.\n\"\"\"",
  "page": null,
  "source": "Theorem:\ngiven:\n. G ::= (X, *, e) is \\group\n. x, y1, y2 \"in\" G\nwhere:\n. x * y1 = e\n. y1 * x = e\n. x * y2 = e\n. y2 * x = e\nthen: y1 = y2\nDocumented:\n. called: \"Uniqueness of Inverses\"\n. description: \"If $y_1$ and $y_2$ both act as inverses for $x$, then\n                $y_1 = y_2$.\"\nProof: \"\"\"\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ both satisfy the equations for an inverse of $x$:\n$x * y_1 = e = y_1 * x$ and $x * y_2 = e = y_2 * x$.\nUsing the identity property and associativity:\n$$ y_1 = y_1 * e = y_1 * (x * y_2) = (y_1 * x) * y_2 = e * y_2 = y_2. $$\nThus any two elements satisfying the inverse equations must coincide.\n\"\"\"\nId: \"a27fd487-85b4-4f18-b0ab-4be63db69f43\"",
